  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f(x)=\\int_0^x\\left(t+\\sin \\left(1-e^{\\prime}\\right)\\right) d t, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then, $\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{f(x)}{x^3}$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "5XPhj",
      "text": "$-\\frac{1}{6}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "EfGNs",
      "text": "$\\frac{2}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "GIXOI",
      "text": "$-\\frac{2}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "8Lrld",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{6}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{f(x)}{x^3}$<br />Using L Hopital Rule.\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{f^{\\prime}(x)}{3 x^2}=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{x+\\sin \\left(1-e^x\\right)}{3 x^2}$ (Again L Hopital)\nUsing L.H. Rule\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& =\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{-\\left[\\sin \\left(1-e^x\\right)\\left(-e^x\\right) \\cdot e^x+\\cos \\left(1-e^x\\right) \\cdot e^x\\right]}{6} \\\\\n& =-\\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
